---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";

const title = "Buddhist Sources and Citations";
const description =
  "How Echo Buddha references Buddhist teachings, translations, paraphrases, scripture, commentary, and further reading.";
const canonicalPath = "/buddhist-sources-and-citations/";
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: title,
  description,
  url: `${SITE.url}${canonicalPath}`,
  publisher: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: SITE.name,
    url: SITE.url
  }
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container prose">
      <a class="back-link" href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a>
      <span class="eyebrow">Sources and citations</span>
      <h1>Buddhist Sources and Citations</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Echo Buddha uses source context to help readers understand where a teaching comes from, while keeping original
        explanation, paraphrase, scripture, and commentary clearly separated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>How Sources Are Used</h2>
      <p>
        Echo Buddha gives beginner-friendly explanations in original language. When a page discusses a traditional
        Buddhist teaching, source notes and further reading links may point to reputable translations, Buddhist study
        resources, recognized institutions, teachers, or practice communities.
      </p>
      <p>
        A source link is not a claim that every view on an external website is endorsed. It is a starting point for
        checking context, comparing translation choices, and continuing study.
      </p>

      <h2>Scripture, Commentary, Paraphrase, and Explanation</h2>
      <p>
        A scripture passage is a traditional text or translation. Commentary explains or interprets a teaching.
        Paraphrase restates an idea in new language. Echo Buddha explanation is original educational writing inspired
        by Buddhist themes and source context.
      </p>
      <p>
        These categories should not be blurred. Echo Buddha should not call an original reflection a scripture
        translation, a Dhammapada quote, a Buddha quote, or a named-person quote unless that origin is verified and
        clearly labeled.
      </p>

      <h2>Translations and Copyright Care</h2>
      <p>
        Buddhist translations differ across traditions, translators, and editions. Echo Buddha avoids long copied
        passages from modern translations unless rights and attribution are clear. When a public-domain or freely
        available source is referenced, the page should still avoid overquoting and should give readers a link for
        further reading where possible.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a translation, edition, or source is uncertain, the page should use cautious language or remain unpublished
        until the uncertainty is resolved.
      </p>

      <h2>Dhammapada and Popular Sayings</h2>
      <p>
        Dhammapada-related pages require extra care because popular internet wording often mixes translation,
        paraphrase, summary, and inspiration. Echo Buddha Dhammapada pages should identify whether a line is a source
        study, a paraphrase, or an original reflection.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same care applies to popular sayings attributed to the Buddha. When an origin cannot be verified, Echo
        Buddha should not publish the saying as a confirmed Buddha quote.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Source, citation, translation, and attribution concerns can be reported through
        <a href="/corrections/">Corrections and Editorial Updates</a>. Include the page URL, the sentence in question,
        and any source information that would help review.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .back-link {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
</style>
